        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          During the Gemini 10 mission (G-10), Command Pilot John W. Young and Pilot Michael Collins performed a space rendezvous and docking, a ''re-boost'' trip into high Earth orbit, a rendezvous with a second target and a spacewalk (extravehicular activity or EVA).  Six of the seven scientific experiments were completed successfully.  Of the seven experiments scheduled for Gemini 10, none studied human life sciences.<P>A unique ''double rendezvous'' took place during Gemini 10:  the Gemini 10 spacecraft was first docked to an Agena target vehicle, Agena 10, which had been launched shortly before Gemini 10.  After being docked to the Agena 10 for 39 hours, the crew separated and headed towards the Agena 8 target vehicle; this Agena rocket had been used as a target during Gemini 8 and then left behind in Earth orbit after the Gemini 8 mission.  The G-10 crew approached the Agena 8 as closely as possible, then Astronaut Collins performed a spacewalk to the target using a nitrogen-fueled "space gun" to provide propulsion through space.  Collins became the first human in space to visit and make contact with a free-floating satellite.<P>Several types of photography experiments were conducted on Gemini 10.  Synoptic Terrain Photography was devoted to the study of the Earth terrain, and produced numerous useful pictures usable for geology, geography or oceanography.  Synoptic Weather Photography documented many weather patterns and events for meteorological studies.  The experiment Zodiacal Light and Airglow Photography provided a series of excellent photographs of the planet Venus.  <P>The Ultraviolet Astronomical Photography experiment obtained ultraviolet photographs of stars that had before been possible only from rocket flights.  Since the spacecraft windows did not admit ultraviolet light, the experiment would not have been possible without the EVA capability of the astronauts.  During the three trials of this experiment on Gemini flights, considerable ultraviolet information was obtained and was especially useful in planning future ultraviolet experiments for manned flights.<P>The Gemini micrometeorite experiments passively collected data on micrometeorites while attached outside the Gemini 8 Agena target vehicle.  They were exposed to open space for four months in orbit until  recovered by the crew of G-10.  Any microscopic meteorite impact craters left on the collection surface were analyzed to assess any potential harm that may be caused by micrometeorite damage to spacecraft or spacesuits during EVAs.  The Gemini Micrometeorite Collection, which was to be attached to the G-10 spacecraft, was not completed successfully during the G-10 mission.  <P>The Gemini Ion Wake Measurement experiment was conducted during the G-10 and G-11 missions to investigate a method for mapping the actual wake of a vehicle.  A great deal of ambient data on ion wake measurement was obtained during G-10.  <P>Continuous, real-time physiological information on all Gemini crewmembers was monitored via telemetered data and voice transmissions.  Data was provided by a two-lead electrocardiogram, an impedance pneumograph measuring respiration, an oral thermistor measuring body temperature, and a blood pressure measuring system.  All of this hardware, including signal conditioners, was mounted on a biosensor harness that fit under the astronauts' flight suits.  Additionally, some inflight film footage was utilized for postflight assessment of crew health.<END>
        </div>

